# Phase 2 daily panel outputs are EVIDENCE. They must be stored byte-for-byte.
#
# This repository runs with core.autocrlf=true, which normalises CRLF to LF when
# a file is staged and converts back on checkout. For source that is harmless.
# For a captured panel it is not: the committed blob would not be the bytes the
# panel actually wrote, so a SHA-256 taken of the capture at collection time
# would not match the SHA-256 of what the repository stores, and neither would
# match what a fresh clone checks out. That breaks the only property these files
# exist to have -- being the unaltered record of a scheduled capture.
#
# -text disables all end-of-line conversion in both directions for these paths.
# It does not mark them unreadable or undiffable; it only stops git rewriting
# their bytes. Applied to the panel's three output shapes and to the completion
# marker, which is hashed alongside them.
#
# Discovered 2026-08-25 while recovering the 2026-08-24 capture: staging the
# artifacts produced blobs whose SHA-256 differed from the files on disk. The
# 2026-08-23 checkpoint committed in aa4e297 was normalised the same way, before
# this rule existed; it is left as committed rather than rewritten, since
# rewriting history to change stored evidence is worse than recording that the
# earlier file was normalised.
# NOTE: gitattributes patterns are whitespace-separated, so a pattern containing
# the directory "Live Signal Engine" cannot be written literally -- git parses the
# text after the space as an attribute name and rejects the line. These filename
# patterns are globally unique to the panel, so matching them at any depth is both
# correct and unambiguous.
GST_Phase2_DailyPanel_TestRun_*.csv    -text
GST_Phase2_DailyPanel_TestRun_*.json   -text

# The unattended runner is PINNED BY CONTENT HASH in the scheduled task's
# --runner-sha256 argument, and check_code_integrity() hashes the file AS IT
# EXISTS ON DISK. With core.autocrlf=true and no attribute, git stores this
# file with LF and checks it out with CRLF, so the on-disk hash depends on how
# the file happened to land rather than on what is committed:
#
#   stored blob (LF)      be5afc86aa85fe95fec40869d9f0cfbeb36ead1ed582c15df78ddd36ce28f06c
#   CRLF checkout         c27ebf5d2e17c2a65ba1b59e30d4ad919ffb075e4b5ab199b2c548ea4f31e497
#
# The currently live worktree holds the LF form only because that file has sat
# there unmodified since it was authored and has never been re-materialised by
# a checkout. A fresh clone would produce the CRLF form and the registered pin
# would no longer match, refusing the next cycle with EXIT_INTEGRITY(14).
#
# "text eol=lf" makes the checkout deterministic: the file is normalised to LF
# in the object store and written to the working tree with LF regardless of
# core.autocrlf or core.eol, so the on-disk SHA-256 equals the committed blob's
# SHA-256 on every machine and every clone. Verified by
# test_phase2_runner_pin_reproducibility.py.
#
# Scoped to this exact path on purpose. This is NOT a repository-wide
# line-ending change; no other file's stored bytes are affected.
tools/run_phase2_daily_panel_unattended.py    text eol=lf
